{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And he who sprinkles the waters of impurity shall wash his garments</b> etc. The verse is elliptical, and this is what it says: and he who sprinkles the waters of impurity, and he who touches the waters of impurity, shall wash his garments and also become impure until evening." "<b>And he who touches the waters of impurity shall become impure.</b> A refutation of the heretics: that the cow renders impure all who engage with it — the reason is because it is prepared outside the camp, and if it did not render impure all who engage with it, we are concerned lest people who make themselves out to be pious come and take from its ashes and sprinkle upon themselves for no purpose, and say: we wish to be pure. Therefore Scripture decreed that all the pure persons who touch it shall become impure. And if you ask: if the one who sprinkles is impure, is it not all the more so <b>he who touches the waters of impurity shall become impure</b> — why is this stated? Because one does not punish by inference; Scripture wrote for you <b>and he who touches the waters of impurity shall become impure.</b> I do not thereby reject the words of our Sages, for all of them are truth. But I wonder at what we learned in tractate Parah, chapter 3: the opening of the Temple courtyard was prepared, a shallow pit for a sin-offering, and they bring a male from the flock, and tie a cord between its horns, and tie a stick and intertwine it at the head of the cord, and throw it into the pit, and strike the male, and it jerks backward, and one takes and consecrates enough so that it is visible on the surface of the waters. Upon this the paytan Elazar HaKalir founded his poem in the section of the red cow: 'They shall bring a horned ram into the pit, upon its head a cord and a whip — purification for the purification of this keeper of vineyards.' For this implies according to the plain sense of Scripture that everyone who sprinkles the waters of impurity requires sprinkling, and how would the last one who sprinkles become pure if not by this procedure."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {1 "The phrase translated 'sin-offering' (חטאת) in the tractate Parah quotation refers not to an animal sacrifice but to the ritual preparation pit; the term is carried over mechanically from the Hebrew as it functions in that mishnaic context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:18:49.658206785Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}